1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,550
You recreate movement and jumping from a game you love.

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:08,850
But playing your version feels unexpectedly different.

3
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:10,585
You transferred the visible features,

4
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:14,650
but may have missed what players noticed and decided.

5
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:18,285
Jumps in Super Meat Boy, dashes in Hollow Knight,

6
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:19,630
and movement through portals

7
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:22,930
all involve different decisions.

8
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:25,945
Instead of recreating a finished game,

9
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:28,530
let us observe small actions and change their conditions

10
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:31,730
to build rules for our own game.

11
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:36,120
In these new examples, look for a decision
rather than a feature name.

12
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:40,960
Into the Breach shows movement cells and enemy
attack directions together.

13
00:00:41,340 --> 00:00:46,060
The question is what to protect and where to
move, beyond simply moving forward.

14
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:51,140
In Noita, notice how terrain and nearby
materials change after a projectile passes.

15
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:56,620
Watching damage alone and watching the route
left afterward are different observations.

16
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:01,040
One fire button may involve both a target and
an approach route.

17
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:05,480
Copying the striking effect does not
automatically create those choices.

18
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:09,700
Record the information you saw, the action you
chose, and the result left on screen.

19
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:13,820
The original small diagram is a starting point
for combining them into your own rule.

20
00:01:14,567 --> 00:01:18,397
First, break an enjoyable moment into a small action.

21
00:01:18,487 --> 00:01:21,937
Go beyond saying it looks beautiful or feels good.

22
00:01:22,327 --> 00:01:24,597
Before a jump, read the platform and gap.

23
00:01:24,727 --> 00:01:26,892
In the air, adjust your landing.

24
00:01:26,907 --> 00:01:30,172
After landing, check the outcome.

25
00:01:30,187 --> 00:01:34,192
Write down noticing, choosing, acting, and recognizing the outcome.

26
00:01:34,207 --> 00:01:36,317
This gives your analysis a clear target.

27
00:01:36,567 --> 00:01:39,337
In Hollow Knight, look beyond the presence of a dash button.

28
00:01:39,507 --> 00:01:43,812
Examine when and where a dash is used, and where it leaves the player.

29
00:01:43,827 --> 00:01:47,637
Copying a character color does not reproduce those decisions.

30
00:01:48,333 --> 00:01:52,933
Choose just one movement and attack in Into
the Breach.

31
00:01:53,293 --> 00:01:58,653
Separate reading the attack direction, moving
the mech, and the changed enemy position.

32
00:01:58,953 --> 00:02:04,393
Writing only 'hit an enemy' can miss how
displacement changes the next situation.

33
00:02:04,913 --> 00:02:10,533
In Noita, observe one projectile's path
instead of the entire exploration.

34
00:02:10,973 --> 00:02:15,373
What was the target, what changed, and where
could the character move afterward?

35
00:02:15,373 --> 00:02:19,713
In FTL, observe the ship's rooms, crew, and
power indicators together.

36
00:02:20,053 --> 00:02:24,853
Choose one immediate state to attend to
instead of the broad feature 'control a ship.'

37
00:02:25,393 --> 00:02:28,013
You do not need to cut every game into an
equal duration.

38
00:02:28,573 --> 00:02:33,133
Find a moment that includes the information
before a choice and its result afterward.

39
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:38,183
Second, turn a feeling into conditions you can examine.

40
00:02:38,313 --> 00:02:41,663
Saying a dash feels exciting does not specify what to build.

41
00:02:41,813 --> 00:02:43,523
Is it the speed?

42
00:02:43,633 --> 00:02:45,458
Passing through danger?

43
00:02:45,473 --> 00:02:47,943
Being able to try again quickly?

44
00:02:48,093 --> 00:02:50,423
The same action may be enjoyable for several reasons.

45
00:02:50,593 --> 00:02:53,823
Portal is more than an effect showing someone entering a doorway.

46
00:02:53,893 --> 00:02:55,458
You consider where entrances and exits should go

47
00:02:55,473 --> 00:02:58,323
to move yourself and objects to the intended place.

48
00:02:58,413 --> 00:03:00,698
These examples are not ready-made answers for your game.

49
00:03:00,713 --> 00:03:03,798
Use them to describe decisions, risks, and outcomes

50
00:03:03,813 --> 00:03:06,103
in an enjoyable moment more precisely.

51
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:10,460
Record visible changes separately from your
explanation of the fun.

52
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:14,480
A changed piece of terrain in Noita is an
observable event.

53
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:18,500
'I liked having a new route' is an idea you
want to investigate.

54
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:23,680
Separate the number of routes from the new
option to avoid danger.

55
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:27,660
FTL's power and room states can be observed in
the same way.

56
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:31,840
Was the appeal the amount of information, or
having several needs at once?

57
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:36,780
An edited introduction cannot establish a
player's thoughts or the internal code.

58
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:41,140
Bring the question it raises into the original
controlled test.

59
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:45,780
Keep the feeling while making the condition
you compare smaller.

60
00:03:46,747 --> 00:03:50,597
Now change one condition at a time in an original prototype.

61
00:03:51,007 --> 00:03:52,912
Keep the jump and platforms alike,

62
00:03:52,927 --> 00:03:56,737
but make the landing zone wide on the left and narrow on the right.

63
00:03:57,167 --> 00:03:58,792
Both versions use a jump button,

64
00:03:58,807 --> 00:04:02,797
but a narrow landing asks for more attention to timing and adjustment.

65
00:04:03,087 --> 00:04:06,337
Keeping other conditions alike makes comparison easier.

66
00:04:06,627 --> 00:04:09,232
Next, compare immediate retries

67
00:04:09,247 --> 00:04:12,177
with a brief wait after failure.

68
00:04:12,527 --> 00:04:16,452
Does it help players learn the rule, or simply add waiting?

69
00:04:16,467 --> 00:04:19,572
Changing speed, artwork, and rewards together

70
00:04:19,587 --> 00:04:22,057
makes their effects hard to separate.

71
00:04:22,447 --> 00:04:24,652
Before producing a polished screen,

72
00:04:24,667 --> 00:04:27,417
use a small test to examine your idea.

73
00:04:28,697 --> 00:04:32,247
FTL shows several rooms and state indicators
during a battle.

74
00:04:32,487 --> 00:04:35,967
It can prompt a question about which
information to check first.

75
00:04:36,327 --> 00:04:41,307
Two different battles edited together are not
a controlled comparison.

76
00:04:41,587 --> 00:04:46,327
If enemies, ships and equipment all change,
several factors may explain a difference.

77
00:04:46,667 --> 00:04:52,067
The original jump test changed only the
platform width or restart wait.

78
00:04:52,327 --> 00:04:56,027
Keeping other factors fixed makes the changed
condition easier to examine.

79
00:04:56,027 --> 00:05:01,747
For a material-rich world like Noita, a small
experiment might begin with one material.

80
00:05:02,267 --> 00:05:07,107
Use the striking example to find a question
and the small test to investigate it.

81
00:05:07,427 --> 00:05:10,927
Connecting their roles turns observation into
design work.

82
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:16,310
Third, preserve a decision and combine it with different rules.

83
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:19,165
Imagine enjoying the decision to wait briefly

84
00:05:19,180 --> 00:05:22,110
before crossing a gap in one move.

85
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:25,710
That decision can appear beyond a jumping game.

86
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:28,265
Try a puzzle about passing through a moving doorway,

87
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:32,970
or a stealth activity that permits movement during a light signal.

88
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:37,425
Our three prototypes reuse similar shapes for jumping, passage, and movement.

89
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:41,245
Change what the player waits for and where failure returns them,

90
00:05:41,260 --> 00:05:42,830
and the required choices change too.

91
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:46,090
These new tests are not automatically fun.

92
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:48,545
Ask players where they looked

93
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:51,770
and when they intended to press, then revise the rules.

94
00:05:52,433 --> 00:05:55,993
When making another game, more than the
scene's appearance can be retained.

95
00:05:56,433 --> 00:06:00,673
A position choice in Into the Breach asks
which cells will be affected next.

96
00:06:01,073 --> 00:06:04,873
Noita's changing space can prompt a prediction
about the route left after an action.

97
00:06:05,413 --> 00:06:07,513
This does not make the two games' rules
identical.

98
00:06:07,853 --> 00:06:12,493
Choose the decision of predicting an outcome
and put it into another test situation.

99
00:06:12,893 --> 00:06:18,213
A moving-door game or a light-timing game can
also ask that question.

100
00:06:18,386 --> 00:06:24,353
FTL's competing needs might become a choice of
where to send limited help first.

101
00:06:24,653 --> 00:06:28,573
New rules can be tested without borrowing the
original game's name or artwork.

102
00:06:28,833 --> 00:06:32,793
Ask actual players whether that decision works
in the new situation.

103
00:06:33,573 --> 00:06:36,458
When analyzing a game, go beyond feature names.

104
00:06:36,473 --> 00:06:38,543
Describe one moment of play.

105
00:06:38,733 --> 00:06:42,983
What did you notice, choose, and recognize as the result?

106
00:06:43,173 --> 00:06:44,838
Change one condition,

107
00:06:44,853 --> 00:06:47,963
then place a decision you want to preserve in a new situation.

108
00:06:48,173 --> 00:06:52,183
Check whether your explanation holds up in the actual test.

109
00:06:52,353 --> 00:06:54,738
Instead of importing a finished answer from a favorite game,

110
00:06:54,753 --> 00:06:57,978
learn about the experience it creates.

111
00:06:57,993 --> 00:07:00,263
This may be more useful for your own design.

112
00:07:00,453 --> 00:07:03,558
What single moment did you enjoy recently?

113
00:07:03,573 --> 00:07:06,923
Start with the choice you made, and turn it into a small test.

114
00:07:07,100 --> 00:07:10,540
Finally, organize the notes you can leave
after observing an actual scene.

115
00:07:10,605 --> 00:07:14,960
For Into the Breach, record a position choice
after reading an attack direction.

116
00:07:15,180 --> 00:07:20,440
For Noita, observe the environmental change
and what remains for the next movement.

117
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:25,400
For FTL, record which simultaneous state you
wanted to attend to first.

118
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:28,700
Then choose one small condition that could
investigate that explanation.

119
00:07:29,300 --> 00:07:34,040
Show the changed test and ask where the player
looked and when they decided.

120
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:39,140
An idea from a trailer and an actual player's
answer are different evidence.

121
00:07:39,720 --> 00:07:43,600
If they differ, find the missed information or
result before adding more features.

122
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:48,800
Observing, testing and rebuilding helps you
create rules for your own game.
